//? Title: Gauss–Newton: Local Least Squares and Full-Step Limits
//? Subtitle: Exact updates, convergence rates and the NIST Misra1a benchmark
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Fitting a nonlinear curve means choosing parameters that make its predictions close
to measured responses. A good starting guess can lead to an accurate fit in a few
updates, while a poor one can make the error much larger. Gauss–Newton repeatedly
solves a linear least-squares approximation without computing residual Hessians.
We derive its direction, work through tiny exact examples, compare quadratic and
linear local convergence, and show a full step that increases error dramatically.
A three-parameter sinusoid retains the original report's generated-data check.
NIST's observed 14-row adsorption dataset then provides certified parameter and SSE
references. Analytic and scaled central-difference fits match those references;
raw-rate differences reveal unit sensitivity. Local linear accuracy, numerical
stopping, parameter identification and
predictive validity are evaluated as separate questions.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Linearize the residual vector

Given coordinates \(x_i\), observed responses \(y_i\), model \(m(p,x)\) and parameter
vector \(p\in\mathbb R^n\), define \(r_i(p)=m(p,x_i)-y_i\). For \(m\) observations,
minimize the unweighted sum of squared residuals, or its half-scaled equivalent:

$$
 S(p)=\sum_{i=1}^m r_i(p)^2,\quad \phi(p)=\tfrac12 S(p),\qquad
 \nabla\phi=J^Tr,\quad
 \nabla^2\phi=J^TJ+\sum_i r_i\nabla^2r_i,
 \quad J_{ij}=\frac{\partial r_i}{\partial p_j}.
$$
\alttext(The objective is the sum of squared model-minus-observed residuals. Half that objective has gradient J transpose r and Hessian J transpose J plus a residual-weighted sum of residual Hessians.)

Full Newton uses both Hessian terms. Gauss–Newton replaces the residual function by
its first-order approximation and solves

$$
 \delta=\arg\min_d\|r+Jd\|_2^2,\qquad
 J^TJ\delta=-J^Tr,\qquad p_{k+1}=p_k+\delta_k.
$$
\alttext(The Gauss–Newton direction minimizes the squared norm of the linearized residual. Its normal equations equate J transpose J times the step to minus J transpose r. The basic algorithm takes the entire step.)

The normal equations describe the solution algebraically; the native implementation
solves the rectangular problem by Householder QR. It first scales each nonzero
Jacobian column to unit Euclidean norm, solves for a scaled step, then restores the
original parameter units. This avoids explicitly forming \(J^TJ\), whose condition
number is the square of that of a full-rank \(J\). Column normalization helps unit
imbalance but cannot separate parallel columns or repair an uninformative experiment.
Numerically rank-deficient scaled Jacobians are rejected explicitly.

The TikZ diagram shows a curved residual as a function of one parameter. Solving
its tangent model produces a candidate, not a promise about the actual residual at
that candidate. Regeneration needs LaTeX, PGF/TikZ, standalone, Latin Modern and
dvisvgm. Documentation export uses the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/gn_tangent_geometry.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/gn_tangent_geometry.tikz > build/gn_tangent_geometry.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/gn_tangent_geometry.svg build/gn_tangent_geometry.dvi > build/gn_tangent_geometry.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local residual-tangent diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local residual-tangent diagram.
// \image(figures/gn_tangent_geometry.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The linearized residual predicts a zero that differs from the true nonlinear root.)
\alttext(A blue parabola represents r(p)=p squared minus two. At p=one its residual is minus one. A red tangent crosses zero at p=one and a half, while the nonlinear root is square root of two. The actual residual at the tangent prediction is one quarter.)

>> A descent direction can still produce an uphill full step

For a full-column-rank Jacobian and a nonzero least-squares direction,

$$
 \nabla\phi^T\delta=(J^Tr)^T\delta
 =-\delta^TJ^TJ\delta=-\|J\delta\|_2^2<0.
$$
\alttext(The directional derivative of half-SSE along a nonzero full-rank Gauss–Newton direction is minus the squared norm of J times the direction, so sufficiently small positive steps lower the objective.)

This proves decrease for sufficiently small positive step lengths. It does not prove
that the full multiplier one is sufficiently small. The basic routine takes full
steps without a line search, trust region, damping, parameter bounds or acceptance
test; increasing SSE remains visible in the trace. Levenberg–Marquardt modifies the
local problem with damping, but that is a different algorithm and is not silently
substituted here. This report concerns unweighted least squares; changing the loss
or observation weights changes the problem.

>> Local convergence depends on both rank and residual curvature

Suppose \(p_*\) is a stationary solution with full-column-rank \(J_*\), and the residual
Jacobian is sufficiently smooth nearby. For the exact-derivative iteration map,

$$
 T'(p_*)=-(J_*^TJ_*)^{-1}C_*,\qquad
 C_*=\sum_i r_i(p_*)\nabla^2r_i(p_*).
$$
\alttext(The derivative of the Gauss–Newton iteration at a stationary point is minus inverse J transpose J times the residual-weighted residual Hessian.)

If the iteration derivative is a local contraction, convergence is locally linear.
Small residuals help only when the Jacobian remains well-conditioned and residual
curvature is controlled. At a zero-residual solution \(C_*=0\), giving local quadratic
convergence under the usual smoothness and rank assumptions. A small residual alone
is not a guarantee. Finite-difference errors and rounding eventually disturb these
asymptotic rates. Nonzero residuals can also have \(C_*=0\); they do not invariably
force a merely linear rate.

>> Native contracts and numerical work

`gauss_newton(model,x,y,start,max_iter,tol)` uses central finite differences. The
analytic overload additionally accepts a callback returning one parameter-ordered
Jacobian row. Coordinates, responses, parameters and model/derivative outputs must
be finite; the input vectors are nonempty, lengths match, and observations must be
at least as numerous as parameters. Missing values are rejected rather than omitted.
Callbacks must return repeatable values and be defined at every visited point;
callback exceptions propagate. Central differences also need both nearby perturbed
parameters to remain in the domain. Their step is
\(h_j=\sqrt[3]{\epsilon}\max(1,|p_j|)\), using the actual representable plus-minus span.
Analytic derivatives avoid these perturbations and their truncation/cancellation error.

The routine preserves the original call signature and the original `params`,
`iterations`, `sse` result fields. `gauss_newton_linearize()` exposes the local
residuals, Jacobian, proposed step, current gradient norm and predicted SSE.
`trace()` records the initial state and every completed update. `sse` and residuals
always correspond to the returned parameters, including when the update budget is
exhausted. `iterations` counts completed updates; a zero budget evaluates only the
initial state and its proposed next step.

Stopping checks whether the proposed next-step norm is at most `tol`, a positive
absolute tolerance in the supplied parameter units. `step_tolerance_met` names this
specific event. A small step is not a proof of a small gradient, a minimum, unique
parameters or a global optimum. Changing parameter units changes this stopping
criterion even though the exact full least-squares direction transforms consistently.
`gradient_inf` is the infinity norm of \(J^Tr\), the gradient of half-SSE in those units.
This uses the supplied or approximate Jacobian: it is an approximate gradient with
finite differences. The trace's `step_norm` describes the incoming completed update; `next_step_norm`
and `linearized_sse` describe the proposed outgoing update at that row.

After column normalization, QR uses rank threshold
\(\max(m,n)\epsilon\max_j|R_{jj}|\). Its numerical-rank check concerns the local
linearization, not global identification. A rank failure raises an exception even
at a small residual. A nonfinite full-step evaluation is also reported as an error;
there is no backtracking recovery.

The existing QR implementation forms an explicit \(m\)-by-\(m\) orthogonal factor.
Each evaluated state therefore costs \(O(mn^2+m^2n)\) linear-algebra work and
\(O(m^2+mn)\) temporary storage, plus \(m(1+2n)\) scalar model calls for central
finite differences, or \(m\) model and \(m\) analytic-row calls. The trace requires
\(O((k+1)n)\) storage after \(k\) updates. A compact QR could avoid the explicit-Q
cost for large datasets; the present implementation is suited to small dense fits.

> Simple examples

>> Three exact points: linear least squares in one full update

Let \(m(p,x)=p_0+p_1x\), coordinates \((-1,0,1)\), responses \((0,2,4)\), and start
\((0,0)\). The residual is \((0,-2,-4)\), with Jacobian rows \((1,x_i)\). Therefore
\(J^TJ=\operatorname{diag}(3,2)\) and \(-J^Tr=(6,4)\), so \(\delta=(2,2)\).
The linearization is exact everywhere: one full update recovers the line, with
zero residual in exact arithmetic. The trace evaluates the final state before
reporting its SSE. Its proposed next step is essentially zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_linear_step.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<double> x = {-1, 0, 1}, y = {0, 2, 4};
  const auto model    = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double x) { return p[0] + p[1] * x; };
  const auto jacobian = [](const std::vector<double>&, double x) {
    return std::vector<double>{1, x};
  };
  const auto local = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton_linearize(model, jacobian, x, y, {0, 0});
  const auto fit   = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, x, y, {0, 0}, 1);
  auto       left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.points(x, y, "observed", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .line({-1, 1}, {0, 4}, "one-step fit", {37, 99, 235})
      .title("One update recovers the line")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("response")
      .y_limits(-1, 6);
  right.points(x, local.residuals, "initial residual", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .points(x, fit.residuals, "final residual", {37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .title("The linear model is exact")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("model minus observed")
      .y_limits(-4.5, 2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gn_linear_step.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("x", x);
  table.add_column("observed", y);
  table.add_column("initial_residual", local.residuals);
  table.add_column("J_intercept", local.jacobian.col(0));
  table.add_column("J_slope", local.jacobian.col(1));
  std::print(
      "Local step: ({:.8f}, {:.8f}).\n\nLocal problem:\n\n{}\n\nEvaluated parameter "
      "states:\n\n{}\n",
      local.step[0],
      local.step[1],
      table.to_md(8),
      fit.trace().to_md(8));
}
```
