\caption(A linear residual model is solved in one full update, with final-state diagnostics evaluated at the returned parameters.)
\alttext(The observed three points and fitted line appear on the left. Initial and final residuals appear on the right. Tables show every Jacobian row, the exact update and the initial/final parameter states.)

>> One nonlinear residual: the tangent predicts the next parameter

Fit \(m(p,0)=p^2\) to one response \(y=2\), starting from \(p=1\). The residual is
−1 and its derivative is 2. Thus \(\delta=-r/J=1/2\), and the first parameter is 1.5.
The tangent predicts residual zero, but the actual residual there is .25, giving
SSE .0625. Subsequent updates converge to the positive root \(\sqrt2\); a negative
start can reach the negative root, so zero residual does not identify the sign.

The first program below intentionally allows just one update. Its final SSE and
residual describe \(p=1.5\), and its stopping flag remains false because the proposed
next update has magnitude \(1/12\). The longer trace shows that evaluating the
returned state and satisfying a stopping test are different matters.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_scalar_tangent.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto model    = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double) { return p[0] * p[0]; };
  const auto jacobian = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double) {
    return std::vector<double>{2 * p[0]};
  };
  const auto first = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, {0}, {2}, {1}, 1, 1e-14);
  const auto fit   = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, {0}, {2}, {1}, 20, 1e-14);
  auto       left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> p, r, tangent;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 100; ++i) {
    const double u = .6 + 1.3 * i / 100;
    p.push_back(u);
    r.push_back(u * u - 2);
    tangent.push_back(-1 + 2 * (u - 1));
  }
  left.line(p, r, "nonlinear residual", {37, 99, 235})
      .line(p, tangent, "tangent at p=1", {220, 38, 38})
      .line({.6, 1.9}, {0, 0}, "zero", {100, 116, 139})
      .points({1, 1.5}, {-1, .25}, "", {37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .title("Tangent zero versus actual residual")
      .x_label("parameter p")
      .y_label("residual p squared minus two")
      .y_limits(-2, 4);
  const auto trace = fit.trace();
  right
      .line(
          trace.numeric_column("iteration").values(),
          trace.numeric_column("p0").values(),
          "iterates",
          {37, 99, 235})
      .line(
          {0, double(fit.iterations)},
          {std::sqrt(2.), std::sqrt(2.)},
          "positive root",
          {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Full updates approach the positive root")
      .x_label("completed updates")
      .y_label("parameter p")
      .y_limits(.95, 1.8);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gn_scalar_tangent.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;
  fmt.digits     = 8;
  fmt.scientific = {"sse", "gradient_inf", "step_norm", "next_step_norm", "linearized_sse"};
  std::print(
      "One-update budget: completed {}; step tolerance met {}; returned SSE {:.8f}.\nLonger fit: "
      "completed {}; step tolerance met {}.\n\n{}\n",
      first.iterations,
      first.step_tolerance_met,
      first.sse,
      fit.iterations,
      fit.step_tolerance_met,
      trace.to_md(fmt));
}
```
