\caption(Exact scalar recurrences distinguish quadratic zero-residual convergence from a one-third linear error ratio.)
\alttext(Logarithmic parameter-error traces show the two local rates. The second panel shows eleven error ratios for the nonzero-residual problem against the one-third limit. Tables contain unfloored early errors and the nonzero-residual final state.)

>> Overshoot and local rank failure are different problems

Fit \(p^2\) to one response one, starting from \(p=.1\). The direction is
\((1-p^2)/(2p)=4.95\), so the full step reaches 5.05. The local linear residual is
zero there, but the actual SSE jumps from .9801 to 600.37250625. The direction was
a descent direction; the chosen full step was too long. Later full steps recover
in this particular example, which is no guarantee for other starts or models.

Starting at exactly zero is a different failure: the Jacobian vanishes, so the local
least-squares direction is not identified. The native routine raises a rank error.
Two identical Jacobian columns likewise prevent a unique full-rank step. Adding
a stopping test or a more accurate linear solve does not resolve these cases.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_overshoot.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto model    = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double) { return p[0] * p[0]; };
  const auto jacobian = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double) {
    return std::vector<double>{2 * p[0]};
  };
  const auto fit   = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, {0}, {1}, {.1}, 20, 1e-14);
  const auto trace = fit.trace();
  auto       left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> parameter, loss, logloss;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 120; ++i) {
    const double p = 5.3 * i / 120;
    parameter.push_back(p);
    loss.push_back(std::pow(p * p - 1, 2));
  }
  for (const auto& row : fit.history)
    logloss.push_back(std::log10(std::max(1e-16, row.sse)));
  left.line(parameter, loss, "actual SSE", {37, 99, 235})
      .points(
          {.1, 5.05},
          {fit.history[0].sse, fit.history[1].sse},
          "first full update",
          {220, 38, 38},
          6)
      .title("The first full step overshoots")
      .x_label("parameter p")
      .y_label("sum of squared residuals")
      .y_limits(0, 1100);
  right.line(trace.numeric_column("iteration").values(), logloss, "actual SSE", {37, 99, 235})
      .points(trace.numeric_column("iteration").values(), logloss, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .title("Recovery here does not imply robustness")
      .x_label("completed updates")
      .y_label("log10 SSE, floor -16")
      .y_limits(-16, 8);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gn_overshoot.svg");
  std::print("Zero-start rank failure: ");
  try {
    (void)dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, {0}, {1}, {0});
  } catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {
    std::print("{}\n", e.what());
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;
  fmt.digits     = 8;
  fmt.scientific = {"sse", "gradient_inf", "step_norm", "next_step_norm", "linearized_sse"};
  std::print("\nAll visited states (no objective-increase filtering):\n\n{}\n", trace.to_md(fmt));
}
```
