> NIST Misra1a: observed data with a certified fit

>> Model, units and starting values

`dm::datasets::misra1a()` contains fourteen measured pressure/volume pairs from
Misra's 1978 monomolecular-adsorption study, in NIST source order, with no missing
values. The source does not specify measurement units, so we retain its numeric
units without inventing labels. NIST uses the two-parameter saturation model

$$
 y=A(1-e^{-b x})+\varepsilon,\qquad
 q=1000b,\quad m(A,q,x)=-A\,\operatorname{expm1}(-qx/1000),\qquad
 J=\left(1-e^{-qx/1000},\; A\frac{x}{1000}e^{-qx/1000}\right).
$$
\alttext(The adsorption model rises towards amplitude A with rate b. The report fits q equal to one thousand times b and uses expm1 to evaluate one minus the exponential accurately. Its Jacobian contains the amplitude derivative and the scaled-rate derivative.)

Fitting \((A,q)\) avoids placing the tiny raw rate beside an amplitude near 239.
The nonlinear model and unweighted objective are unchanged; the absolute parameter
step tolerance is expressed in these chosen units. NIST's two starts become
\((500,.1)\) and \((250,.5)\). The certified estimates are
\(A=238.94212918\), \(b=.00055015643181\), with SSE .12455138894.
Use all fourteen observations for numerical certification, with no test split and
no parameter or model selection. Agreement with these reference numbers measures
solver accuracy on this benchmark, not prediction on unseen experiments.

The distant start can take an unphysical negative-amplitude intermediate step and
greatly increase SSE. No positivity constraint or acceptance rule prevents that.
Both analytic-start traces below are retained in full. Compare their final values
with a central-difference fit from the second start. A stopped flag describes the
specified step tolerance; inspect the gradient and certified objective alongside it.

A fourth fit uses the raw rate b with default central differences. Here the fixed
scale floor makes the finite-difference perturbation about 1.1% of b near the
solution, versus about .0011% of q. Its amplitude differs by roughly 2.86e-5
from the certified value despite nearly identical fitted curves and SSE. QR column
normalization cannot remove derivative truncation error already present in the
Jacobian. Analytic derivatives or a suitable parameterization address it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_misra1a.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d = dm::datasets::misra1a();
  const auto x = d.numeric_column("pressure").values(), y = d.numeric_column("volume").values();
  const auto model = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double x) {
    return -p[0] * std::expm1(-p[1] * x / 1000);
  };
  const auto jacobian = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double x) {
    const double t = x / 1000;
    return std::vector<double>{-std::expm1(-p[1] * t), p[0] * t * std::exp(-p[1] * t)};
  };
  const auto far       = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, x, y, {500, .1}, 100, 1e-10),
             near      = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, jacobian, x, y, {250, .5}, 100, 1e-10),
             numeric   = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(model, x, y, {250, .5}, 100, 1e-9);
  const auto raw_model = [](const std::vector<double>& p, double x) {
    return -p[0] * std::expm1(-p[1] * x);
  };
  const auto raw_numeric = dm::algorithms::gauss_newton(raw_model, x, y, {250, .0005}, 100, 1e-9);
  auto       left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> grid, curve;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 200; ++i) {
    grid.push_back(800. * i / 200);
    curve.push_back(model(near.params, grid.back()));
  }
  left.points(x, y, "observed", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .line(grid, curve, "fitted adsorption", {37, 99, 235})
      .title("NIST observed adsorption data")
      .x_label("pressure, source units")
      .y_label("volume, source units")
      .y_limits(0, 130);
  right.points(x, near.residuals, "model minus observed", {37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .line({50, 800}, {0, 0}, "zero", {100, 116, 139})
      .title("Residuals at the fitted solution")
      .x_label("pressure, source units")
      .y_label("volume residual, source units")
      .x_limits(50, 800)
      .y_limits(-.25, .45);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gn_misra1a.svg");
  const std::vector<const dm::algorithms::GaussNewtonResult*> fits = {
      &far, &near, &numeric, &raw_numeric};
  const std::vector<std::string> names = {
      "analytic, NIST start 1",
      "analytic, NIST start 2",
      "central, NIST start 2",
      "central, raw b"};
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < fits.size(); ++j) {
    auto row = fits[j]->trace().tail(1).select(
        {"iteration", "sse", "gradient_inf", "next_step_norm", "p0"});
    row.add_column("fit", std::vector<std::string>{names[j]});
    row.add_column("b", std::vector<double>{fits[j]->params[1] / (j == 3 ? 1 : 1000)});
    row.add_column(
        "step_tolerance_met", std::vector<double>{fits[j]->step_tolerance_met ? 1. : 0.});
    table = table.ncols() ? table.concat_rows(row) : row;
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;
  fmt.digits     = 11;
  fmt.scientific = {"sse", "gradient_inf", "next_step_norm", "b"};
  std::print(
      "Start-2 analytic fit: A={:.11f}, b={:.14f}, SSE={:.14f}.\n\n",
      near.params[0],
      near.params[1] / 1000,
      near.sse);
  std::print(
      "All fourteen measured observations:\n\n{}\n\nFinal fits (p0=A; reported rate in original b "
      "units):\n\n{}\n\nFull "
      "analytic trace from start 1:\n\n{}\n\nFull analytic trace from start 2:\n\n{}\n",
      d.to_md(2),
      table.to_md(fmt),
      far.trace().to_md(fmt),
      near.trace().to_md(fmt));
}
```
