\caption(All measured rows are used to compare numerical estimates with the NIST reference; the distant-start overshoot is retained.)
\alttext(The left panel shows fourteen observed pressure/volume pairs and the fitted saturation curve, using source units. The right shows fitted-minus-observed residuals. Tables contain all observations, both analytic-start and central-difference final fits, and both complete analytic trajectories including any SSE increases.)

> Conclusion

Gauss–Newton uses least-squares structure to obtain a local direction without
residual Hessians. QR improves the numerical linear solve, while full-step overshoot
and rank failure remain distinct nonlinear and identification concerns. The exact
scalar recurrences show both quadratic and linear convergence under their stated
conditions, and the generated sinusoid checks a multivariate parameter fit.

NIST supplies observed data and certified parameter/SSE references for a direct
numerical accuracy comparison. The scaled analytic fit returns A=238.94212917890,
b=.00055015643181 and SSE=.12455138894441, matching the certified values to their
reported precision. Default differences in raw rate units show a small derivative
bias that becomes visible in the parameter table. The distant-start trace demonstrates why a successful
final answer does not imply safe intermediate steps. Step-size stopping, low SSE,
stationarity, unique parameters and predictive validity each require their own
assessment. Difficult fits may need better parameterization, improved experimental
information, or an explicitly safeguarded method.

> References

- B. W. Rust (2002), “Fitting Nature's Basic Functions Part III: Exponentials,
  Sinusoids and Nonlinear Least Squares”, Computing in Science & Engineering 4(4).
  https://www.nist.gov/publications/fitting-natures-basic-functions-part-iii-exponentials-sinusoids-and-nonlinear-least.
- D. Bindel (2026), “Nonlinear least squares”, Cornell CS 4220 lecture notes,
  https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs4220/2026sp/lec/2026-04-10.html.
  Derivation of the local error map and conditions on residual size and Jacobian rank.
- NIST/ITL Statistical Reference Datasets, “Misra1a”, observed adsorption data,
  starting values and certified estimates; Misra (1978),
  https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/strd/nls/data/LINKS/DATA/Misra1a.dat.

